VulkanLumen — Stage 2: original Cornell scene + direct area lighting

The public system API is unchanged. vulkan-experiment/main.cpp remains the
permanent test harness; rendering implementation lives in lumen/.

Scene: exact geometry and base materials from
xt9y/vulkan-experiment/path-tracer-bak:
  - Cornell dimensions: width 5.55, depth 5.592, height 5.488
  - White reflectance (.725, .710, .680)
  - Red reflectance (.630, .065, .050) on the right wall (x = 5.55)
  - Green reflectance (.140, .450, .091) on the left wall (x = 0)
  - Short block: original rotated base, height 1.65
  - Tall block: original rotated base, height 3.30
  - Emissive ceiling rectangle: origin (2.13,5.484,2.27),
    size 1.30 x 1.05, radiance (17,16,14)
  - 16 original analytic faces (96 raster vertices), no front wall

Implemented:
  create()   Allocates Cornell vertex/face SSBO data, HDR color/depth targets,
             rasterization pipeline, descriptors and composite pipeline.
  record()   Rasterizes visible geometry; Lambertian direct area-light energy
             and surface-to-light visibility are computed in the fragment shader
             using 2x2 deterministic area-light samples.
  compose()  Displays the HDR target using the existing tone map.
  reset() / resize()   Release/recreate GPU resources safely.
  implemented()        True after pipelines have been initialized.

For speed, shadow rays use conservative bounding boxes for the two interior
Cornell blocks, followed by intersections against their 10 analytic faces.
Room boundaries are convex and cannot occlude an interior point's segment
to the ceiling emitter. No GPU BVH, noise, TAA, SVGF or denoising exists.

Stage 2 intentionally has only direct lighting (one light bounce).
The previous path tracer also computes indirect diffuse light; red/green
color bleeding onto white surfaces will not yet match that reference.

Later stages:
  3. General scene ray tracing via VulkanBVH / compute traversal
  4. Surface cache and card-based material/lighting lookup
  5. World-space radiance probes and cached GI
  6. Screen-probe gather
  7. Screen tracing with scene-ray fallback
  8. Dynamic invalidation and multi-bounce illumination
  9. Reflections
  10. Frame budgets, GPU profiling and M1 performance scaling

Test on macOS/M1:
  cd vulkan-experiment
  git pull --ff-only
  rm -f c.lock
  c build run vulkan-experiment

Expect the original two rotated boxes, corresponding red/green walls,
bright ceiling emitter, soft-ish shadows and direct diffuse illumination.
WASD + mouse controls remain unchanged.

Please report a screenshot, FPS and any compiler/shader output before Stage 3.
On Retina displays the shadow shader may be GPU-heavy. If it is, the next
optimization should use shadow-map sampling or a cheaper light-visibility
representation, not a denoiser.
